<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pharnaces II of Pontus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 97–47 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Pharnaces II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pharnaces II was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a>. In 63 BCE he led the revolt that drove his father to suicide and was rewarded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> with the Bosporan Kingdom. During the Roman civil war he tried to reconquer Pontus, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> defeated him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zela">Zela</a> in 47 BCE, the victory reported as 'veni, vidi, vici', and he was killed soon after fighting his rebel governor Asander.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pharnaces-ii-of-pontus.jpg" alt="Pharnaces II of Pontus" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pharnace II (roi du Pont). Autorité émettrice de monnaie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monnaie_-_Stat%C3%A8re,_Or,_Incertain,_Bosphore_Cimm%C3%A9rien,_Pharnace_II_-_btv1b11317510r_(1_of_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
